    "q-142071": {
      "dom_id": "step-q142071",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-11y+30)$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-11y+30\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+30\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-11y+30) & =-({(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-142071-a",
