    "q-140407": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140407",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y-21$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y+21)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y+21\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+21\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $21$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y-21 & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-140407-a",
