    "q-140530": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140530",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+32x+40$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+8x+10)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-6\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+8x+10) & =4((x+4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-24\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
