    "q-140375": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140375",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}+10x-\\frac{11}{6}$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{11}{36}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}) & =6({(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
