    "q-218758": {
      "dom_id": "step-q218758",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Rewrite $10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}$ by completing the square.\n\n$10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, we take out a factor of $10$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{100}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}) & =10({(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}) \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+10\\cdot \\frac{3}{50} \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
