    "q-140532": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140532",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-40x-60$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+8x+12)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+8x+12) & =-5((x+4)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
