    "q-52544": {
      "dom_id": "step-q52544",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+6y+1$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{3}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}) & =-3({(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-52544-a",
